{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     services/onesignal — web push, with the service-worker constraint a
     Ghost theme cannot wish away.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Renders nothing unless @custom.onesignal_app_id is set.

     ⚠ THE CONSTRAINT, PROMINENTLY. OneSignal's default setup expects
     OneSignalSDKWorker.js at the SITE ROOT (/OneSignalSDKWorker.js). A
     Ghost theme cannot put a file there: Ghost serves theme files only
     under /assets/, and everything else at the root is Ghost's own
     routing. Skip this paragraph and push "works" in the console but no
     worker ever registers, which is the worst kind of broken: silent.

     THE WORKAROUND USED HERE is OneSignal's own, from their service-worker
     documentation: `serviceWorkerPath` (a root-relative path to the .js
     file, no leading slash) plus `serviceWorkerParam` (the registration
     scope) let the worker live in any same-origin subdirectory. This
     partial points both at /assets/onesignal/, which means the theme must
     ship ONE extra file:

         assets/onesignal/OneSignalSDKWorker.js
         └── importScripts("https://cdn.onesignal.com/sdks/web/v16/OneSignalSDK.sw.js");

     Ghost serves /assets/ files same-origin with a JavaScript content
     type, which satisfies the browser's service-worker rules; the scope
     sits at the worker's own directory, so no Service-Worker-Allowed
     header is needed — and a push worker needs no page-controlling scope
     anyway. The alternative workaround is a CDN rule (a Cloudflare Worker
     or origin rule serving the file's CONTENT at /OneSignalSDKWorker.js —
     a redirect is not enough, service-worker registration refuses
     redirects). It works, but it moves a theme concern into
     infrastructure nobody diffs; the serviceWorkerPath route keeps the
     whole feature inside the theme and is what OneSignal documents for
     exactly this situation.

     ⚠ THE WORKER URL IS PERMANENT. Browsers re-fetch the worker from the
     URL it was registered at; OneSignal's docs say to keep a superseded
     URL alive for at least a year or existing subscribers stop receiving
     pushes. /assets/onesignal/ is therefore a commitment, not a default —
     do not rename the directory once real subscribers exist.

     NOT BEHIND THE ANALYTICS CONSENT GATE, deliberately. Push has its own,
     stronger consent moment: the browser's native permission prompt, which
     no script can bypass and which is the reader answering THIS question
     specifically. Gating the SDK behind an analytics banner would double-
     ask without adding protection. The SDK loads defer from OneSignal's
     CDN — a ~0.6 KB detector that pulls the real ~43 KB (wire) bundle
     after parse — so it cannot block first paint.

     OneSignal's dashboard settings decide the prompt experience (their
     slide-down "soft prompt" before the native one is strongly
     recommended — a native prompt on first visit is how a site earns a
     permanent block). That is configuration, not theme code.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
{{#if @custom.onesignal_app_id}}
<script src="https://cdn.onesignal.com/sdks/web/v16/OneSignalSDK.page.js" defer></script>
<script>
    window.OneSignalDeferred = window.OneSignalDeferred || [];
    window.OneSignalDeferred.push(async function (OneSignal) {
        await OneSignal.init({
            appId: "{{@custom.onesignal_app_id}}",
            serviceWorkerPath: "assets/onesignal/OneSignalSDKWorker.js",
            serviceWorkerParam: { scope: "/assets/onesignal/" }
        });
    });
</script>
{{/if}}
